  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates the lifecycle and membrane trafficking of a potassium channel in muscle-cell membrane, emphasizing how channel abundance at the sarcolemma is controlled by insertion, recycling, and degradation. Newly made channel protein appears to be transcribed in the nucleus, processed through the ER and Golgi, and then delivered in vesicles toward the cell surface. A RAB11-associated anterograde pathway brings Kv1.5 to the membrane, where it is inserted and shown as a modified surface channel labeled Kvβ1.3, with nearby symbols suggesting regulatory phosphorylation and nitric oxide-related signaling.\n\nThe right side shows the opposing process: channels are removed from the membrane by endocytosis into intracellular vesicles. From there, the internalized protein can either recycle back toward the surface, again involving RAB11, or be routed through endosomal compartments marked by RAB4/RAB5 and RAB7. These pathways lead either back into the biosynthetic/recycling system or onward to degradation. Final degradation is depicted through lysosomal and proteasomal routes.\n\nOverall, the image conveys that functional channel expression at the membrane is dynamically balanced by biosynthesis and forward trafficking versus endocytosis, recycling, and destruction.",
